  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 159 AM CDT Tue May 26 2026\n\nMost of the convection that is forecast to move through the area \nTuesday night into early Wednesday morning will be east of the area \nby daybreak, although a few showers and thunderstorms could linger \nin our eastern counties. Additional isolated to scattered showers \nand thunderstorms are possible during the day, mainly across the \neastern half of the area. Highs on Wednesday will be in the upper \n70s to lower 80s. \n\nOn Thursday, a large upper level low will be centered across central \nCalifornia and northern Nevada, with a weak upper level trough \nacross northeast Texas. Weak upper level ridging is then forecast \nacross West Texas. This will result in warm and mainly dry \nconditions on Thursday. Although an isolated afternoon shower or \nthunderstorms is possible, mainly across our eastern counties, \nmainly dry conditions are forecast to prevail. \n\nFriday into the weekend, the aforementioned upper level low will \nweaken and lift north, while an upper level trough approaches, \nflattening out the ridge that was previously across West Texas. \nThis will result in increasing rain chances, especially Saturday \ninto Sunday. The GFS even develops a weak upper level low across \nthe region which would add additional support for shower and \nthunderstorm development. For now, have kept PoPs in the low to \nmedium range (30-60%). Highs Friday and Saturday will be in the \nmid to upper 80s, with highs on Sunday in the low to mid 80s.",
